VGE shader ownership and engine separation
==========================================

Source proposal
  Rendering.ShaderOwnership.Proposal.md (sibling of this file) is authoritative.
  Status: Phases 1-6 complete; independent executable ownership, registry/reload and ordered shutdown validated.
  Earlier local qualification is recorded below; live liquid acceptance exposed a camera-order defect.
  Live acceptance rejected the aliased-matrix correction; its package is superseded.
  Shared capture now runs after native camera preparation at Before order 0.001;
  focused qualification passed and the user confirmed the live fix and remaining acceptance scenarios.
  Second review and independent completion audit passed 2026-10-10; final acceptance is complete.

Scope and shared contracts
  Establish IGpuProgram with default implementation methods, implemented by independent
  GpuProgram and renamed GpuComputeProgram families. Remove VGE executable dependence on
  engine ShaderProgram inheritance, IShaderProgram, ShaderRegistry and CurrentShaderProgram.
  Establish explicit VGE declaration, preparation, activation, reload and retirement ownership.
  Resolve the cached pbr_final registration failure without constructing placeholder engine stages.

  Preserve StateCache, GraphicsCommandContext, GraphicsPipeline, EngineBoundaryScope and existing
  program scopes. No shared GpuShader base, parallel activation stack, current-owner dictionary,
  second context registry, per-frame native scans or blanket invalidation.
  Keep IShaderSubmissionTarget narrow; keep mutable state in concrete owners, not the interface.
  Reuse SPIR-V loading, binary caches, ShaderLinkBatch, generated bindings and resource deletion.
  Keep compute feature-owned where appropriate; do not force global compute registration.

  Non-goals: replace terrain rendering, engine traversal/culling, framebuffer allocation, shader
  compilation tools or native shader patching; support arbitrary engine-shader interruption;
  merge graphics and compute preparation backends or rename every feature shader class.
  Engine integration stays in adapters. Borrowed resources keep their existing ownership.

Execution and evidence
  Phases are dependency-ordered review increments of one migration. Intermediate states are not
  independently shippable while liquid inputs, reload invalidation or retirement remain incomplete.
  Temporary compatibility paths must have removal tasks below; none may become a second authority.
  Run every build/test tool through a subagent as required by repository instructions.
  Keep source/installed-IL evidence, compilation, focused unit tests, headless GPU integration,
  packaged-shader verification and user-run game acceptance distinct. Do not launch the game.
  Record concise receipts with the relevant task and linked contracts; no completion marks based
  solely on source inspection where executable or live evidence is required.

Resolved decisions for dependent implementation
  ☑ Choose internal/public IGpuProgram accessibility and restricted state/hook access.
    - Use internal IGpuProgram with public GpuProgram/GpuProgramLayout and internal IShaderSubmissionTarget.
      Do not expose mutable lifecycle state or internal layout solely to enable default methods.
  ☑ Specify distinct default workflow entry points, family hooks and required concrete facades.
    - Prevent same-slot recursive dispatch; preserve protected Submit and authored overrides.
  ☑ Select explicit compute reload policy: retain feature-owned reconstruction initially, or
    implement retained rebuild descriptions and transactional replacement before promising it.
    - RetryPreparation alone is not invalidation of an already valid compute executable.
  ☑ Choose the liquid pool interception after verifying installed call sites: narrow input adapter
    at selected getter calls, or direct translation of the supported uniform operations.
  ☑ Specify safe-boundary rejection/defer policy for actively borrowed executable replacement,
    duplicate declarations, registry lifetime and actual shutdown/context-deletion ordering.

Phase 1: Pin ownership contracts and migration coverage
  Outcome: interfaces, call-site obligations and validation boundaries are concrete enough to migrate.
  Proposal: Evidence and current dependencies; Proposed ownership model; Compatibility;
    Resolved migration contracts; Migration coverage and evidence.

  ☑ Inventory inherited API and ambient ownership consumers.
    - Trace GpuProgram partials, GpuComputeProgram, GpuShaderPrograms, GraphicsCommandContext,
      EngineBoundaryExecution, direct Use/TryUse/UseScope and low-level compute consumers.
    - Include LiquidGraphicsSubmission, MeshDataPoolManager.Render, LiquidPoolSubmissionHook,
      SceneColorProgramBindings, EngineRenderScopes and test/diagnostic uniform adapters.
    - Audit OwnedShaderSubmissionHook/GpuProgramStopHook and automatic frame/light inputs;
      distinguish VGE compensation from native terrain/atmosphere/material/particle behavior.
  ☑ Confirm installed engine registration, Use/Stop/Dispose, pool input/finally restoration and
    reload call ordering against current assembly/API evidence, without running the game.
  ☑ Define declaration identity, installed executable revision/asset generation and originating
    native context identity using RenderContextRegistry; forbid numeric-ID-only ownership.
  ☑ Resolve the decisions above in the proposal or linked owning contracts before their consumers.
    - Define binding-only restoration versus immediate input replay and next-draw publication.
    - State which incoming engine/raw/compute footprints remain unsupported at boundaries.
  ☑ Map existing tests to interface dispatch, cache loading, scopes, generators, liquids, registry,
    reload and disposal; identify fixtures that currently hide registration side effects.
  ☑ Completion: each inherited/ambient dependency has a replacement owner or retained integration
    role, open decisions have an execution gate, and the migration has a source-backed test matrix.

  Evidence: Rendering.ShaderOwnership.Proposal.md / Resolved migration contracts and
    Migration coverage and evidence (installed 1.22.7 IL receipt, source disposition and test map).
  Traceability: inventory -> Evidence/Compatibility -> dependency disposition; installed behavior
    -> Evidence -> dated module/IL receipt; identity/decisions -> Proposed ownership model ->
    resolved contracts; verification coverage -> Acceptance -> existing-test and gap matrix.
  Verification: source/installed-IL inspection and second review completed 2026-10-10; independent
    audit-stage-completion passed with no material gaps. No build, test or game execution; those
    validate later implementation and remain open.

Phase 2: Establish IGpuProgram workflows and compute naming
  Depends on Phase 1. Outcome: shared behavior and generated owners have a verified VGE contract.
  Proposal: Shared interface and default implementation; Compute family rename and migration.

  ☑ Introduce IGpuProgram default workflows for guarded preparation, activation/publication,
    scoped use and terminal lifetime; reuse the narrow IShaderSubmissionTarget view.
    - Keep lifecycle flags and CPU-block ownership in stable per-owner state with restricted access.
      Keep settings, retained generated values and installed generations with their family owners.
    - Separate workflow methods from preparation, Submit and disposal hooks; retain required
      concrete facades and bridge protected Submit without recursive interface dispatch.
    - Check guards before cleanup-owning activation; a rejected recursive call must not unwind
      an enclosing submission. Publish inputs even when the program ID is already bound.
    - Make retirement idempotent; reject mutation/disposal during publication and prevent revival.
    - Share CPU uniform registration/write guards and terminal release; preserve extra-resource
      disposal hooks, failure cleanup and compute diagnostic Dispatch overrides.
  ☑ Rename GpuComputeShader and its file to GpuComputeProgram; update family constructors,
    derived declarations, type references, fixtures, tests and documentation references.
    - Keep GpuComputePipeline as the single native compute executable owner and preserve direct
      and indirect dispatch contracts. Do not add common-workflow double deletion.
  ☑ Update RuntimeSubmissionEmitter, BindingReader and InterfaceBindingReader recognition.
    - Resolve the existing pipeline-field assumption deliberately; preserve concrete eligibility,
      abstract intermediate families, authored Submit overrides and property mutation guards.
    - Verify runtime/offline generated shells without assuming default methods become class members.
  ☑ Validate through concrete, IGpuProgram and IDisposable entry points: sequencing, failed
    publication cleanup, repeated disposal, recursion rejection, owned CPU blocks and generated inputs.
    - Keep current family preparation differences explicit; no compute invalidation no-op contract.
    - If graphics still needs an engine transition path before Phase 4, keep it temporary and
      explicitly isolate/remove it in Phases 3-4 rather than weaken the final interface contract.
  ☑ Completion: generated families and focused dispatch/lifetime checks pass; compute family naming
    is migrated, native ownership is singular, and remaining engine transition dependencies are listed.

  Traceability: workflows/lifetime -> proposal Shared interface and default implementation,
    Resolved migration contracts / Interface visibility, dispatch and retained state -> IGpuProgram,
    GpuProgramLifetime and family facades; guard/retirement regressions in GpuProgramWorkflowTests,
    GpuComputeProgramWorkflowTests, GpuProgramUseScopeTests and ShaderDemandPreparationTests.
    Rename/generation -> proposal Compute family rename and migration; Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md /
